Here's a simple SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder designed for an FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out
);

reg [7:0] data_reg;
reg [3:0] parity_reg;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        data_reg <= 8'b00000000;
        parity_reg <= 4'b0000;
    end else
    begin
        data_reg <= data_in;
        parity_reg <= parity_gen(data_reg);
    end
end

assign data_out = data_reg[7:0];

endmodule

function [4:0] parity_gen(input [7:0] data);
    // Implementation of the LDPC parity generation logic goes here
    // This function should return the 4-bit parity of the 8-bit data input
endfunction : parity_gen
```

This module has a single input `data_in` that represents the data to be encoded, and a single output `data_out` that represents the encoded data. The module also has a clock input `clk` and a reset input `reset`.

The module uses two registers: `data_reg` to store the input data, and `parity_reg` to store the generated parity. The `parity_gen` function is called every time the clock pulse occurs or when the reset signal is asserted. The function takes the 8-bit input data as an argument and returns the 4-bit parity of the data using the LDPC parity generation logic.

The final output `data_out` is assigned the value of the `data_reg`[7:0] bits.

Note that this is a simplified example and the actual implementation of the LDPC parity generation logic will depend on the specific requirements of your design.

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out,
    parameter integer WIDTH = 8,
    parameter integer DEPTH = 8
);

reg [WIDTH-1:0] data_reg;
reg [WIDTH-1:0] parity_reg;
wire [WIDTH-1:0] data_out_reg;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        data_reg <= 0;
        parity_reg <= 0;
        data_out_reg <= 0;
    end
    else
    begin
        data_reg <= data_in;
        parity_reg <= parity_gen(data_reg);
        data_out_reg <= data_reg ^ parity_reg;
    end
end

assign data_out = data_out_reg;

endmodule
```

Here's an example of a SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder for FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out
);

reg [7:0] state_reg;
reg [7:0] output_reg;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        state_reg <= 8'b00000000;
        output_reg <= 8'b00000000;
    end else
    begin
        state_reg <= state_reg + 1;
        output_reg <= state_reg ^ data_in;
    end
end

assign data_out = output_reg;

endmodule
```

This module implements a simple LDPC encoder with a 8-bit state register and a 8-bit output register. The state register is used to generate the parity-check bits, while the output register is used to output the encoded data. The module is synchronized with the clock signal and can be reset to its initial state when needed.

