The recursive formula for the sequence T(n) is:
T(n) = T(sqrt(n)) + 5n

To solve this using the telescoping method, we need to express the sum of terms from T(1) to T(n) as a single term. We do this by subtracting each term from the next term and adding it to the previous term:

T(n) - T(sqrt(n)) = 5n
T(sqrt(n)) - T(sqrt(sqrt(n))) = 5sqrt(n)
T(sqrt(sqrt(n))) - T(sqrt(sqrt(sqrt(n)))) = 5sqrt(sqrt(n))
...
T(1) - T(sqrt(sqrt(sqrt(sqrt(n)))} = 5\sqrt{n}

Now, we can add up all these differences to get the sum of the terms from T(1) to T(n):

T(n) - T(1) = (T(sqrt(n)) - T(sqrt(sqrt(n)))) + (T(sqrt(sqrt(n))) - T(sqrt(sqrt(sqrt(n))))) + ... + (T(\sqrt{n}) - T(\sqrt{n})) + 5\sqrt{n}
T(n) - T(1) = 5\sqrt{n} + 5\sqrt{n} + 5\sqrt{n} + ... + 5\sqrt{n} + 5\sqrt{n}
T(n) - T(1) = 5n\sqrt{n}

Therefore, the sum of the terms from T(1) to T(n) is given by:

T(n) = T(1) + 5n\sqrt{n}

Since T(1) = 0, we have:

T(n) = 5n\sqrt{n}

This is the solution to the recursive formula using the telescoping method.

