Here's a simple MATLAB code that identifies the tense of a given sentence:

```matlab
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Define regular expressions for each tense
    past_simple = '^have|had';
    past_continuous = '^been|being';
    future_simple = '^will|shall';
    future_continuous = '^be|being';
    present_simple = '^am|are|is|was|were';
    present_continuous = '^be|being';
    conditional_simple = '^would|could|should';
    conditional_continuous = '^would|could|should|be|being';

    % Check if the sentence matches any of the regular expressions
    if strcmp(sentence, past_simple) || strcmp(sentence, past_continuous)
        tense = 'past';
    elseif strcmp(sentence, future_simple) || strcmp(sentence, future_continuous)
        tense = 'future';
    elseif strcmp(sentence, present_simple) || strcmp(sentence, present_continuous)
        tense = 'present';
    elseif strcmp(sentence, conditional_simple) || strcmp(sentence, conditional_continuous)
        tense = 'conditional';
    else
        tense = 'unknown';
    end
end

% Test the function with the given sentence
sentence = 'I would like to have a meeting today';
tense = identify_tense(sentence);
disp(['The tense of the sentence is ', tense]);
```

This code defines regular expressions for each tense and checks if the given sentence matches any of them. If a match is found, the function returns the corresponding tense. Otherwise, it returns 'unknown'.

