To restrict access to your website only from authorized devices, you can use a combination of server-side scripting and client-side storage. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. **Server-side Scripting**: On your web server, create a script that will handle the device authorization. This script should check if the device is authorized before allowing access to the website. You can use various methods to determine if a device is authorized, such as checking for a unique device identifier, IP address, or using a challenge-response mechanism like CAPTCHA.

2. **Client-side Storage**: Use local storage or session storage on the client-side to store the device's authorization status. This will allow your website to remember the device's authorization status even if the user closes and reopens the browser.

Here's an example of how you can implement this using JavaScript:
```javascript
// Server-side script (e.g., server.php)
<?php
// Check if the device is authorized
if (!is_authorized()) {
    // Redirect the user to an unauthorized page
    header("Location: unauthorized.html");
}
?>

// Client-side script (e.g., index.html)
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Website</title>
    <script>
        // Check if the device is authorized
        function isAuthorized() {
            // Retrieve the device's authorization status from local storage
            var authorizationStatus = localStorage.getItem("authorizationStatus");

            // If the authorization status is not set or is false, redirect the user to an unauthorized page
            if (!authorizationStatus || authorizationStatus === "false") {
                window.location.href = "unauthorized.html";
                return false;
            }

            // If the authorization status is true, allow access to the website
            return true;
        }
    </script>
</head>
<body>
    <!-- Your website content goes here -->
</body>
</html>
```
This example assumes that the server-side script `server.php` checks if the device is authorized and sets the `authorizationStatus` in local storage if it is.

