Here's an example of a simple MATLAB simulation of natural selection based on the given scenario:

```matlab
% Define initial population
population_size = 100;
brown_rabbits = population_size * 0.7;
white_rabbits = population_size - brown_rabbits;

% Create cell arrays for rabbits
brown_rabbits_cell = {'Brown', zeros(brown_rabbits, 1)};
white_rabbits_cell = {'White', zeros(white_rabbits, 1)};

% Combine cell arrays into one
population_cell = [brown_rabbits_cell white_rabbits_cell];

% Simulate natural selection
for i = 1:100
    % Survival probability based on fur color
    survival_probability_brown = 0.8;
    survival_probability_white = 0.6;

    % Randomly select survivors
    brown_survivors = randperm(brown_rabbits, round(survival_probability_brown * brown_rabbits));
    white_survivors = randperm(white_rabbits, round(survival_probability_white * white_rabbits));

    % Update population
    population_cell(brown_survivors) = {'Brown', ones(length(brown_survivors), 1)};
    population_cell(white_survivors) = {'White', ones(length(white_survivors), 1)};
end

% Display final population distribution
disp(['Final population distribution:']);
disp(population_cell);
```

This code simulates a population of 100 rabbits, with 70% being brown and 30% being white. The survival probability for brown rabbits is set at 0.8, while that of white rabbits is set at 0.6. The code then iterates through 100 generations, randomly selecting survivors based on their fur color and updating the population accordingly. The final population distribution will show a higher proportion of brown rabbits compared to white rabbits, reflecting the advantageous trait of blending in with the environment.

Sure! Here's a simple MATLAB code that simulates a basic model of natural selection. In this example, we'll be working with a population of rabbits, where the fitness of each rabbit is determined by its fur color.

```MATLAB
% Define the initial population
population = [0 1 0; % Rabbit 1: Brown, Fitness = 0
             1 0 0; % Rabbit 2: White, Fitness = 0
             0 1 0]; % Rabbit 3: Brown, Fitness = 0

% Define the number of generations
numGenerations = 10;

% Simulate natural selection
for i = 1:numGenerations
    % Calculate the fitness of each rabbit
    fitness = [1 0 1; % Brown rabbits have higher fitness
               0 1 0; % White rabbits have lower fitness] * population;

    % Select parents based on fitness
    selectedParents = sort(fitness, 'descend');

    % Generate offspring through reproduction
    offspring = zeros(size(selectedParents));
    for j = 1:size(selectedParents, 1)
        offspring(j, :) = selectedParents(j, :);
    end

    % Update the population with offspring
    population = offspring;
end

% Display the final population
disp('Final population:');
disp(population);
