response:
To avoid having your planets too close together, you can introduce a minimum distance between them. You can achieve this by modifying the `generateRandomPlanets` function to check if a potential planet's position is within the minimum distance of any existing planet before adding it to the list. If it is, generate a new random position until one that satisfies the minimum distance requirement is found.

Here's an updated version of the `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const distance = planet.position.reduce((a, b) => Math.sqrt(a - b), Infinity);

      if (distance < minDistance) {
        continue;
      }
    }

    planets.push(planet);
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this modified version, we iterate through all existing planets and calculate the distance between the new planet's position and each existing planet's position. If the distance is less than the specified minimum distance, we skip the new planet and generate a new random position. This process continues until we find a position that satisfies the minimum distance requirement or we reach the maximum number of attempts.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from text. It uses the `string-width` library to measure the width of a string and adjusts the line breaks accordingly.

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import stringWidth from 'string-width';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [lines, setLines] = useState([]);

  const handleTextChange = (event) => {
    const newText = event.target.value;
    const linesArray = newText.split('\n');

    const adjustedLines = linesArray.map((line) => {
      const width = stringWidth(line);
      if (width > 80) {
        return line.replace(/\b\w{1,}(\s*)/g, (match, word, space) => {
          const remainingSpace = 80 - width;
          const truncatedWord = match.slice(0, -remainingSpace);
          return `${truncatedWord} ${space}`;
        });
      }
      return line;
    });

    setLines(adjustedLines);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={text} onChange={handleTextChange} />
      <ul>
        {lines.map((line, index) => (
          <li key={index}>{line}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

