Here's a simple React utility to remove widows. This utility will check if there are any words that are wider than the container width and adjust the font size accordingly.

```javascript
import React, { useRef, useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ children }) => {
  const containerRef = useRef(null);
  const [fontSize, setFontSize] = useState(16);

  const handleResize = () => {
    const containerWidth = containerRef.current.offsetWidth;
    const words = children.split(' ');
    let adjustedFontSize = fontSize;

    for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
      const wordWidth = words[i].getBoundingClientRect().width;

      if (wordWidth > containerWidth) {
        adjustedFontSize -= 1;
        break;
      }
    }

    setFontSize(adjustedFontSize);
  };

  return (
    <div ref={containerRef} onResize={handleResize}>
      {children}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility, simply wrap your content with the `RemoveWidows` component:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <RemoveWidows>
      <p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.</p>
      <p>Sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua.</p>
    </RemoveWidows>
  );
};

export default App;
```

This utility will automatically adjust the font size to prevent widows from occurring. Note that this is a simple approach and may not work perfectly for all cases. For more complex layouts, you might need to use more advanced techniques or libraries.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from text. This utility will break the text into smaller chunks if it detects that a word is being cut off at the end of a line.

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [chunks, setChunks] = useState([]);

  const handleTextChange = (event) => {
    const newText = event.target.value;
    setChunks(removeWidowsFromText(newText));
  };

  const removeWidowsFromText = (text) => {
    const words = text.split(' ');
    const chunkedWords = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
      const currentWord = words[i];
      const nextWord = words[i + 1];

      if (currentWord.length > 0 && nextWord !== undefined) {
        const combinedLength = currentWord.length + nextWord.length;
        const maxWidth = 50; // Adjust this value to control the maximum width of each chunk

        if (combinedLength > maxWidth) {
          chunkedWords.push(currentWord);
          i++;
        } else {
          chunkedWords.push(currentWord + ' ' + nextWord);
        }
      }
    }

    return chunkedWords;
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={text} onChange={handleTextChange} />
      <ul>
        {chunks.map((chunk, index) => (
          <li key={index}>{chunk}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

This component takes a `text` prop and displays it in a text area. It also displays a list of chunks that have been created by breaking the text into smaller pieces to avoid widows. The maximum width of each chunk can be adjusted by changing the value of the `maxWidth` variable.

